# Gavel 24 names three patterns that can hold an err: `(err …)`, an `:err`
# annotation, and a TYPESET with err among its members. The first two carried
# the own-hako guard on every engine. The third did not, on native: the
# typeset branch of `emit_pattern` comes before the annotated branch and
# returns without ever asking `admits_err`, so the guard the third case was
# promised was never emitted.
#
# What that bought a program: a package could rescue its own failure by
# naming a typeset instead of naming err. `own` below answered "the typeset
# arm took it" on native while the interpreter — the oracle — passed the
# failure through, which is a two-universe hole and an engine divergence in
# one shape.
#
# The value line is the other half: the arm still does its job on a member
# that is not a failure, so the guard refuses errs rather than the typeset.
import "std/testing"

type maybe err int

fn as_text reason
  "{reason}"

fn boom what
  err what

fn look _:maybe
  "the typeset arm took it"

fn look v
  "a value: {v}"

own = look (boom "a")

fine = look 7

pub play =
  print "own: {testing/when_failed own as_text}"
  >> print "value: {fine}"
